v
 The project's own program, and the nearest thing Befunge has to a greeter.
 There is no import of any kind, so nothing here can be called from anywhere
 else -- not from a test, not from another file. A program is the only unit
 there is, and `make shell` then `cfunge src/{{PROJECT_SNAKE}}.b98` runs this
 one.
 
 These lines are a comment, and Befunge has no comment syntax. They survive
 because the instruction pointer never reaches them: it starts at the top
 left heading right, the v in column 0 above turns it downward, and it then
 travels down column 0 through the space that each of these lines begins
 with. A space is the no-op. Lose that leading space and the line is code.
 
 So a column is semantics here. The > below turns the pointer right again, it
 runs to the v at the far end, comes back leftward along the row of < pushing
 Hello, Befunge! on to the stack one character at a time, and the last row
 prints until it meets the 0 that was pushed underneath. The pointer's path
 is the control flow and the layout is the program, which is why make fmt
 refuses to do anything at all: moving a cell would change what runs.
>                                                                             v
